Step 2: Option A suggests that lower-level consumers require more energy. While this may be true in terms of total collective demand, it does not directly explain why the number of organisms decreases at higher trophic levels. The focus on lower-level requirements shifts attention away from the energy transfer inefficiency that limits upper levels.

Step 3: Option B states that top-level consumers require more energy. This aligns with ecological principles where higher per-capita energy needs, combined with less available energy at higher trophic levels, mean that fewer individuals can be sustained. This best explains the decrease in numbers from one trophic level to the next in a typical food pyramid."}

